  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1140 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nLarge upper level ridge covering much of the southern US is \nforecasted to split across Texas as an inverted trough moves into \nthe area. At the surface, a weak cold front will sag across Oklahoma \non Thursday, likely reaching the northern Big Country late in the \nafternoon. Better low level moisture plus the focus from the front \nwill allow for at least some convection across the area. As \nmentioned yesterday, RRFS is the most aggressive with the amount of \nconvection, with other CAMs showing some but not as much, down to \nthe NAMNEST which shows very little. Model blends are taking a \nmiddle of the road approach showing bes convective chances across \nthe northern half of the area with the front, and smallest chances \nalong the I-10 corridor. Will stick with this for now and see if the \nCAMs can coalesce around a consensus forecast. Biggest concern out\nof any of this convection may be the lightning threat. Has not\ntaken much lightning lately to get multiple wildfires started, and\nthe more widespread activity on Thursday afternoon/evening will\nproduce a substantial concern for lightning induced wildfires.\n\nAs for temperatures on Thursday, increased cloud cover may be able \nto keep readings a degree or two cooler, but front is now going to \nbring any cooler air to the region. So highs near the 100 degree \nmark still look likely.\n\nUpper level ridge rebuilds slightly to cover the Souther Plains for \nthe weekend into next week, although it does appear a little weaker \nand not directly centered over the area. Temperatures will still be \nhot and for those areas that don't see significant rainfall on \nThursday, the dry soil alone will help boost highs to near 100 \ndegrees. There keeps being some suggestion in the ensembles that the \nridge will eventually break down and settle into a more fall type of \npattern for the middle to end of the month, but not much chance of \nthat before mid next week.",
